"""The usage text of tmux-pane.sh and watch-run.sh, as their bash usage() printed it (generated from the old headers once)."""

TEXT = {'tmux-pane': "# Run commands in tmux side panes, so the person running them can watch live with a real TTY, and block until the main one finishes.\n# Command-agnostic: it knows nothing about juneau-terminal.  For a juneau-terminal script (push.py, release.py, deploy*.py) with a\n# log tail, use watch-run.sh, which wraps this.\n# Requires: python3 3.9+, tmux 3.4+, ps, pgrep, bash (for --pane STRING commands); lsof optional.  Tested on macOS.\n#\n# Usage: tmux-pane.sh [-d DIR] [-n LINES] [-t TITLE] [-k fail|always|never] [-K MIN] [--reap] [--keep-on-cancel] [--read-only]\n#                     [--hide-launcher] [--quiet-exit] [--size N%] [--pane-title T] --pane STRING [[--size N%] [--pane-title T] --pane STRING ...]\n#        tmux-pane.sh [opts] -- CMD [ARGS...]          (one leader pane running the command)\n#        tmux-pane.sh cancel [PANE|TITLE|--all]\n#        tmux-pane.sh list\n#        tmux-pane.sh close [--kept|PANE]\n#        tmux-pane.sh equalize PANE [--closing]\n#        tmux-pane.sh copy PANE|TITLE [-n LINES]\n#        tmux-pane.sh zoom PANE|TITLE\n#\n#   -d DIR    working directory for the commands (default: the current one)\n#   -n LINES  how many trailing leader-pane lines to print when it ends (default 60)\n#   -t TITLE  leader pane title (default: the command name, or `bash` with --pane)\n#   -k KEEP   when to keep the panes open after the leader ends, until Enter is pressed in the leader\n#             or its `[ click here to close ]` button is clicked (SGR mouse reporting, so tmux `mouse on`):\n#             fail (default) only when it exits non-zero, always, or never\n#   -K MIN    kept panes close themselves after MIN minutes (default: they stay until Enter or `close`)\n#   --reap    when the leader ends (or its pane vanishes), also TERM then KILL any process it left running\n#   --keep-on-cancel\n#             a run stopped by `cancel` is handled per -k like any other (default: the panes close, whatever -k says)\n#   --hide-launcher\n#             move the launching pane (when it is this terminal's own foreground process) into a window of its own while the run goes;\n#             it returns when the run ends\n#   --quiet-exit\n#             do not print the pane id, the pane tail and `exit=N` (the caller prints its own ending)\n#   --read-only\n#             ignore keystrokes typed into the panes (`select-pane -d`); `cancel` and `close` still work, but Enter and the button no\n#             longer close a kept leader (no button is shown), so use `close`\n#   --pane STRING\n#             repeatable; STRING runs via `bash -c` in its own pane.  The first --pane is the leader (the caller blocks\n#             on it and exits with its code); the rest are followers, stacked below it in argument order.  Followers\n#             are not tracked or waited on; they close with the leader.  -- CMD is shorthand for one leader pane.\n#   --size N% applies to the next --pane (a follower only): its height as a percentage of the leader's column when it is\n#             created.  The first follower defaults to 40%; later ones without --size split the follower area evenly.\n#\n#   --pane-title T\n#             applies to the next --pane: the title shown on that pane's border line (default: the leader's -t title, and the\n#             first 40 characters of a follower's command)\n#\n# Border titles: when a leader opens in a window whose `pane-border-status` is off, this turns it on (`top`), saving the\n# window's own prior value in the window option @tmux_border_prev, and gives every non-toolkit pane there (the agent pane) an\n# empty pane-border-format so its border line stays blank.  Toolkit panes get a per-pane\n# pane-border-format framing the title as `─┤ title ├─` (with the default [z]/[x] mouse controls on the right) unless they set\n# one already; it goes away with the pane.  When the window's last toolkit pane closes (Enter, click, close,\n# cancel, -K) the saved value and the blanks are put back.  Best effort: a failure only warns.  A window whose border line is\n# already on is left alone.  Killing a leader (say from the border's [x]) closes its followers: the run notices within about\n# 2 s, and each follower also closes itself once its leader pane is gone.\n#\n# The panes open to the right without taking focus.  Every pane gets TMUX_PANE_RUN_ID (the leader's pane id) and\n# TMUX_PANE_RUN_CANCEL (the shell command that cancels the run).  When the leader finishes this prints the pane id, the\n# last LINES lines of the leader pane, the orphan report and `exit=N`, closes the panes unless -k says to keep them, and\n# exits with the leader's code.  Exits 2 when not inside tmux or on a usage error (`-f` was removed).  Exits 129 (after\n# printing `pane gone`) when the leader pane is killed or closed before the command ends.\n#\n# The leader runs in its own process group.  A registry under ${TMPDIR}/tmux-pane/ (one .reg file per pane, plus the\n# .pids list of every descendant seen, sampled about every 2 s) lets `cancel` stop it from any shell: INT to the group,\n# then TERM, then KILL, each after TMUX_PANE_GRACE seconds (default 10), then it reaps stragglers.  A cancelled run\n# closes its panes (leader and followers) after printing its output, unless --keep-on-cancel.  Whenever a run ends,\n# processes it started that are still alive (same PID and same start time, so a reused PID is never hit) are listed with\n# their listening TCP ports as orphans.  Reaping (cancel, or --reap) signals those exact PIDs only, never a pattern.\n# `list` shows this script's panes (id, title, state running|kept|exit N|follower, age); `close` closes finished ones,\n# with their followers.  The window's columns (the pane the script was started from, plus each leader with its followers\n# stacked below) are kept at equal widths: re-sized when a leader opens and when its panes close (best effort, a failed\n# resize only warns).  `equalize PANE` does that by hand for PANE's window; `--closing` leaves PANE itself out.  `copy PANE|TITLE [-n LINES]`\n# puts the pane's text (all its scrollback, or the last LINES lines) on the macOS clipboard via pbcopy, or on stdout when\n# there is no pbcopy; `zoom PANE|TITLE` toggles the pane's zoom.  `cancel`, `list`, `close`, `equalize`, `copy` and `zoom`\n# are reserved first words, so give a command that is named that after `--`.\n#\n", 'watch-run': '# Run a juneau-terminal based script (push.py, release.py, deploy-docs.py) in a watchable tmux pane with its full log tailed in\n# a pane below it, and block until it finishes.  A script that adopts juneau_terminal_scripts.mode launches this itself with\n# --mode split (the engine and this form stay as they are).  A wrapper over tmux-pane.sh that owns the juneau-terminal contract: it\n# chooses the log and result paths itself (so nothing has to guess them), exports them, and prints the result summary.\n# Requires: python3 3.9+, tmux, tmux-pane.sh beside it, perl (for the follower filter).  Tested on macOS.\n#\n# Usage: watch-run.sh [--no-tail] [--no-close-kept] [--no-toolbar] [--keep] [-d DIR] [-n LINES] [-t TITLE] [-k fail|always|never] [-K MIN] [--reap]\n#                     [--keep-on-cancel] [--read-only] -- CMD [ARGS...]\n#\n#   --no-tail  do not open the lower pane running `tail -F` on the full log\n#   --no-close-kept  leave kept panes from earlier runs open (by default they are closed before the new pane opens)\n#   --keep     leave the panes open after the run (what -k always does); by default a run closes every pane and prints the final\n#              board, the result and `exit=N` in the window it was started from, with the full-log path and the last error lines on failure\n#   --no-toolbar  do not open the one-row toolbar pane (see tmux-toolbar.sh: Hide/Show output, Cancel, Close and the run status).\n#                 The toolbar is on by default (set JUNEAU_TERMINAL_TOOLBAR=0 to turn it off everywhere); with --keep or an explicit -k always|fail the panes stay\n#                 open after the run (the toolbar runs as -k always --keep-on-cancel).  It needs\n#                 tmux 3.4 or newer; on an older tmux a warning is printed and the run opens without it.\n#   --check-tmux-version "tmux 3.7c"  exit 0 when that `tmux -V` text is 3.4 or newer (used by the tests)\n#   --print-tail-filter  print the filter the follower pane pipes `tail -F` through, and exit (used by the tests)\n#   The launcher pane (the one this was started in) is moved out of sight while the run goes, when it is the pane\'s own foreground\n#   process, so the board, log and toolbar fill the window; the run\'s panes close when it ends and the launcher comes back.\n#   other options pass straight through to tmux-pane.sh (see its header); -t defaults to the script\'s name\n#\n# Before the pane opens it prints `tail -f <full log>` and `board: <board log>` (relay the first to the person watching).  The\n# logs live in ${JUNEAU_TERMINAL_LOG_DIR:-~/.cache/juneau-terminal} as <stem>-<YYYYMMDD-HHMMSS>-<pid>.log (and .board.log), where\n# <stem> is the script\'s basename; the command gets JUNEAU_TERMINAL_FULL_LOG, JUNEAU_TERMINAL_BOARD_LOG, JUNEAU_TERMINAL_RESULT (a\n# file in a temp dir this removes on exit) and JUNEAU_TERMINAL_CANCEL (the command that cancels the run).  When the command\n# ends, tmux-pane.sh prints the pane tail and `exit=N`; this then prints the result summary (when the command wrote\n# one) and `exit=N` again, so read the last `exit=` line.  Exits with the command\'s code; 2 when not inside tmux.\n#\n'}
